  <h3>Popover position</h3>
  <p>Before anytihng else, lets fix that poor positioning of our popover.</p>
  <p>But firstly let's refresh some fundaments of CSS.</p>
  <p>I'm sure you have used <code>position: absolute</code> or <code>fixed</code> many times. The element that you are
    positioning this way uses closest ansestor with containing block and also can create own inset-modified containing
    block. The similar way we can position our popover with brand new math function <code>anchor()</code>.</p>

  <accordion-panel title="What is a contain block & inset-modified containing block?">
    <p>Containing block is an ancestor element that defines the coordinate system for positioning an element. An
      element's containing block is determined by the nearest ancestor that has a position value other than static
      (i.e., relative, absolute, fixed, or sticky) or certain CSS properties like transform, filter, perspective, etc.
    </p>

    <p>Inset-modified containing block is a special type of containing block that is created when an element has certain
      CSS properties applied to it, such as transform, filter, perspective, or will-change. These properties create a
      new coordinate system for the element and its descendants, allowing for more complex positioning and layout
      behaviors.</p>
  </accordion-panel>

  <h3>Using anchor() for positioning</h3>
  Function <code>anchor()</code> accepts inset properties(<code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>,
  <code>left</code>). Additionaly we can provide which anchor are we using. Function returns <code>&ltlength&gt</code>
  value. Because it's a <code>&ltlength&gt</code>length.

  <p class="warning"><code>anchor()</code> function can be used only in <a
      href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Inset_properties" target="_blank">inset properties</a>
  </p>



